<section number="8.4" title="Add, Subtract, and Multiply Radical Expressions">
  <learning_objectives>
    <objective number="" />
    <objective number="" />
    <objective number="" />
  </learning_objectives>
  <note type="Be Prepared" number="8.10">
    <p>Before you get started, take this readiness quiz.</p>
    <p>Add: $$3x^{2} + 9x - 5 - \left( {x^{2} - 2x + 3} \right).$$
If you missed this problem, review Example 5.5.</p>
  </note>
  <note type="Be Prepared" number="8.11">
    <p>Simplify: $$\left( {2 + a} \right)\left( {4 - a} \right).$$
If you missed this problem, review Example 5.28.</p>
  </note>
  <note type="Be Prepared" number="8.12">
    <p>Simplify: $$\left( {9 - 5y} \right)^{2}.$$
If you missed this problem, review Example 5.31.</p>
  </note>
  <subsection title="Add and Subtract Radical Expressions">
    <p>Adding radical expressions with the same index and the same radicand is just like adding like terms. We call radicals with the same index and the same radicand like radicals to remind us they work the same as like terms.</p>
    <note type="Like Radicals">
      <p>Like radicals are radical expressions with the same index and the same radicand.</p>
    </note>
    <p>We add and subtract like radicals in the same way we add and subtract like terms. We know that $$3x + 8x$$ is $$11x.$$ Similarly we add $$3\sqrt{x} + 8\sqrt{x}$$ and the result is $$11\sqrt{x}.$$</p>
    <p>Think about adding like terms with variables as you do the next few examples. When you have like radicals, you just add or subtract the coefficients. When the radicals are not like, you cannot combine the terms.</p>
    <example number="8.36">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$2\ \sqrt{2} - 7\ \sqrt{2}$$ ⓑ $$5\ \sqrt[3]{y} + 4\ \sqrt[3]{y}$$ ⓒ $$7\ \sqrt[4]{x} - 2\ \sqrt[4]{y}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Example illustrating the subtraction of like radical expressions, with step-by-step explanation.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu} 2\ \sqrt{2} - 7\ \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Since the radicals are like, we subtract thecoefficients.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu}-5\ \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates adding like radicals by combining coefficients, showing an initial expression, the rationale, and the simplified result.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{126mu} 5\ \sqrt[3]{y} + 4\ \sqrt[3]{y}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Since the radicals are like, we add thecoefficients.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{126mu} 9\ \sqrt[3]{y}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="Table presenting the algebraic expression: 7⁴√x - 2⁴√y.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{387mu} 7\ \sqrt[4]{x} - 2\ \sqrt[4]{y}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The indices are the same but the radicals are different. These are not like radicals. Since the radicals are not like, we cannot subtract them.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.71">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$8\sqrt{2} - 9\sqrt{2}$$ ⓑ $$4\sqrt[3]{x} + 7\sqrt[3]{x}$$ ⓒ $$3\sqrt[4]{x} - 5\sqrt[4]{y}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.72">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$5\sqrt{3} - 9\sqrt{3}$$ ⓑ $$5\sqrt[3]{y} + 3\sqrt[3]{y}$$ ⓒ $$5\sqrt[4]{m} - 2\sqrt[3]{m}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>For radicals to be like, they must have the same index and radicand. When the radicands contain more than one variable, as long as all the variables and their exponents are identical, the radicands are the same.</p>
    <example number="8.37">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$2\ \sqrt{5n} - 6\ \sqrt{5n} + 4\ \sqrt{5n}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[4]{3xy} + 5\ \sqrt[4]{3xy} - 4\ \sqrt[4]{3xy}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a radical expression, showing the process of combining like radicals to reach a final value of zero.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 2\ \sqrt{5n} - 6\ \sqrt{5n} + 4\ \sqrt{5n}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Since the radicals are like, we combine them.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 0\ \sqrt{5n}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 0$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Example demonstrating the combination of like radicals.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\sqrt[4]{3xy} + 5\ \sqrt[4]{3xy} - 4\ \sqrt[4]{3xy}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Since the radicals are like, we combine them.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 2\ \sqrt[4]{3xy}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.73">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{7x} - 7\ \sqrt{7x} + 4\ \sqrt{7x}$$ ⓑ $$4\ \sqrt[4]{5xy} + 2\ \sqrt[4]{5xy} - 7\ \sqrt[4]{5xy}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.74">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$4\ \sqrt{3y} - 7\ \sqrt{3y} + 2\ \sqrt{3y}$$ ⓑ $$6\ \sqrt[3]{7mn} + \sqrt[3]{7mn} - 4\ \sqrt[3]{7mn}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Remember that we always simplify radicals by removing the largest factor from the radicand that is a power of the index. Once each radical is simplified, we can then decide if they are like radicals.</p>
    <example number="8.38">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{20} + 3\sqrt{5}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{24} - \sqrt[3]{375}$$ ⓒ $$\frac{1}{2}\sqrt[4]{48} - \frac{2}{3}\sqrt[4]{243}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a radical expression involving addition.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu}\sqrt{20} + 3\ \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radicals, when possible.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu}\sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{5} + 3\ \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu} 2\ \sqrt{5} + 3\ \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu} 5\ \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a radical expression involving the subtraction of cube roots.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu}\sqrt[3]{24} - \sqrt[3]{375}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu}\sqrt[3]{8} \cdot \sqrt[3]{3} - \sqrt[3]{125} \cdot \sqrt[3]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu} 2\ \sqrt[3]{3} - 5\ \sqrt[3]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu}-3\sqrt[3]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step solution demonstrating the simplification and combination of radical expressions involving fourth roots.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\frac{1}{2}\ \sqrt[4]{48} - \frac{2}{3}\ \sqrt[4]{243}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\frac{1}{2}\ \sqrt[4]{16} \cdot \sqrt[4]{3} - \frac{2}{3}\ \sqrt[4]{81} \cdot \sqrt[4]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\frac{1}{2} \cdot 2 \cdot \sqrt[4]{3} - \frac{2}{3} \cdot 3 \cdot \sqrt[4]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\sqrt[4]{3} - 2\ \sqrt[4]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\text{−}\sqrt[4]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.75">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{18} + 6\ \sqrt{2}$$ ⓑ $$6\ \sqrt[3]{16} - 2\ \sqrt[3]{250}$$ ⓒ $$\frac{2}{3}\ \sqrt[3]{81} - \frac{1}{2}\ \sqrt[3]{24}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.76">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{27} + 4\ \sqrt{3}$$ ⓑ $$4\ \sqrt[3]{5} - 7\ \sqrt[3]{40}$$ ⓒ $$\frac{1}{2}\ \sqrt[3]{128} - \frac{5}{3}\ \sqrt[3]{54}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>In the next example, we will remove both constant and variable factors from the radicals. Now that we have practiced taking both the even and odd roots of variables, it is common practice at this point for us to assume all variables are greater than or equal to zero so that absolute values are not needed. We will use this assumption throughout the rest of this chapter.</p>
    <example number="8.39">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$9\sqrt{50m^{2}} - 6\sqrt{48m^{2}}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{54n^{5}} - \sqrt[3]{16n^{5}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step guide on simplifying a mathematical expression involving radical terms, detailing the breakdown of square roots and explaining why the final terms cannot be combined.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 9\ \sqrt{50m^{2}} - 6\ \sqrt{48m^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 9\ \sqrt{25m^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{2} - 6\ \sqrt{16m^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 9 \cdot 5m \cdot \sqrt{2} - 6 \cdot 4m \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 45m\ \sqrt{2} - 24m\ \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The radicals are not like and so cannot becombined.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Illustrates the step-by-step simplification of a mathematical expression involving the subtraction of two cube roots.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\sqrt[3]{54n^{5}} - \sqrt[3]{16n^{5}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\sqrt[3]{27n^{3}} \cdot \sqrt[3]{2n^{2}} - \sqrt[3]{8n^{3}} \cdot \sqrt[3]{2n^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu} 3n\ \sqrt[3]{2n^{2}} - 2n\ \sqrt[3]{2n^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu} n\ \sqrt[3]{2n^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.77">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{32m^{7}} - \sqrt{50m^{7}}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{135x^{7}} - \sqrt[3]{40x^{7}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.78">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{27p^{3}} - \sqrt{48p^{3}}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{256y^{5}} - \sqrt[3]{32y^{5}}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Multiply Radical Expressions">
    <p>We have used the Product Property of Roots to simplify square roots by removing the perfect square factors. We can use the Product Property of Roots ‘in reverse’ to multiply square roots. Remember, we assume all variables are greater than or equal to zero.</p>
    <p>We will rewrite the Product Property of Roots so we see both ways together.</p>
    <note type="Product Property of Roots">
      <p>For any real numbers, $$\sqrt[n]{a}$$ and $$\sqrt[n]{b},$$ and for any integer $$n \geq 2$$</p>
      <equation>$$\sqrt[n]{ab} = \sqrt[n]{a} \cdot \sqrt[n]{b}\quad\text{and}\quad\sqrt[n]{a} \cdot \sqrt[n]{b} = \sqrt[n]{ab}$$</equation>
    </note>
    <p>When we multiply two radicals they must have the same index. Once we multiply the radicals, we then look for factors that are a power of the index and simplify the radical whenever possible.</p>
    <p>Multiplying radicals with coefficients is much like multiplying variables with coefficients. To multiply $$4x \cdot 3y$$ we multiply the coefficients together and then the variables. The result is 12xy. Keep this in mind as you do these examples.</p>
    <example number="8.40">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {6\ \sqrt{2}} \right)\left( {3\ \sqrt{10}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {-5\ \sqrt[3]{4}} \right)\left( {-4\ \sqrt[3]{6}} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step guide to multiplying and simplifying radical expressions, from (6√2)(3√10) to 36√5.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}\left( {6\ \sqrt{2}} \right)\left( {3\ \sqrt{10}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply using the Product Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 18\ \sqrt{20}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 18\ \sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 18 \cdot 2 \cdot \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} 36\ \sqrt{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step multiplication and simplification of cube root expressions.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {-5\ \sqrt[3]{4}} \right)\left( {-4\ \sqrt[3]{6}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply using the Product Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 20\ \sqrt[3]{24}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 20\ \sqrt[3]{8} \cdot \sqrt[3]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 20 \cdot 2 \cdot \sqrt[3]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 40\ \sqrt[3]{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.79">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {3\sqrt{2}} \right)\left( {2\sqrt{30}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {2\ \sqrt[3]{18}} \right)\left( {-3\ \sqrt[3]{6}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.80">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {3\sqrt{3}} \right)\left( {3\sqrt{6}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {-4\ \sqrt[3]{9}} \right)\left( {3\ \sqrt[3]{6}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>We follow the same procedures when there are variables in the radicands.</p>
    <example number="8.41">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {10\ \sqrt{6p^{3}}} \right)\left( {4\ \sqrt{3p}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {2\ \sqrt[4]{20y^{2}}} \right)\left( {3\ \sqrt[4]{28y^{3}}} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step process of multiplying and simplifying a radical expression, showing the intermediate results.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {10\sqrt{6p^{3}}} \right)\left( {4\sqrt{3p}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 40\sqrt{18p^{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 40\sqrt{9p^{4}} \cdot \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 40 \cdot 3p^{2} \cdot \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 120p^{2}\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ When the radicands involve large numbers, it is often advantageous to factor them in order to find the perfect powers.</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step solution for multiplying and simplifying radical expressions involving fourth roots.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {2\sqrt[4]{20y^{2}}} \right)\left( {3\sqrt[4]{28y^{3}}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 6\ \sqrt[4]{4 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 7y^{5}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 6\ \sqrt[4]{16y^{4}} \cdot \sqrt[4]{35y^{}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 6 \cdot 2y\ \sqrt[4]{35y^{}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 12y\ \sqrt[4]{35y^{}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.81">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {6\sqrt{6x^{2}}} \right)\left( {8\sqrt{30x^{4}}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {-4\ \sqrt[4]{12y^{3}}} \right)\left( {\text{−}\sqrt[4]{8y^{3}}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.82">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {2\sqrt{6y^{4}}} \right)\left( {12\sqrt{30y}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {-4\ \sqrt[4]{9a^{3}}} \right)\left( {3\ \sqrt[4]{27a^{2}}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Use Polynomial Multiplication to Multiply Radical Expressions">
    <p>In the next a few examples, we will use the Distributive Property to multiply expressions with radicals. First we will distribute and then simplify the radicals when possible.</p>
    <example number="8.42">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{6\ }\left( {\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{18}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{9}\ \left( {5 - \sqrt[3]{18}} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="This table outlines the sequential steps involved in simplifying a radical expression, from initial multiplication to combining radicals.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{108mu}\sqrt{6}\ \left( {\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{18}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{108mu}\sqrt{12} + \sqrt{108}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{108mu}\sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{3} + \sqrt{36} \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{108mu} 2\sqrt{3} + 6\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like radicals.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{108mu} 8\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression involving cube roots through distribution and simplification.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu}\sqrt[3]{9}\ \left( {5 - \sqrt[3]{18}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu} 5\sqrt[3]{9} - \sqrt[3]{162}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu} 5\ \sqrt[3]{9} - \sqrt[3]{27} \cdot \sqrt[3]{6}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{216mu} 5\ \sqrt[3]{9} - 3\ \sqrt[3]{6}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.83">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{6}\left( {1 + 3\sqrt{6}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{4}\left( {-2 - \sqrt[3]{6}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.84">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{8}\left( {2 - 5\sqrt{8}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{3}\left( {\text{−}\sqrt[3]{9} - \sqrt[3]{6}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>When we worked with polynomials, we multiplied binomials by binomials. Remember, this gave us four products before we combined any like terms. To be sure to get all four products, we organized our work—usually by the FOIL method.</p>
    <example number="8.43">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {3 - 2\sqrt{7}} \right)\left( {4 - 2\sqrt{7}} \right)$$ ⓑ $$\left( {\sqrt[3]{x} - 2} \right)\left( {\sqrt[3]{x} + 4} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step multiplication and simplification of two binomial expressions containing square roots.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {3 - 2\sqrt{7}} \right)\left( {4 - 2\sqrt{7}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 12 - 6\sqrt{7} - 8\sqrt{7} + 4 \cdot 7$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 12 - 6\sqrt{7} - 8\sqrt{7} + 28$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 40 - 14\sqrt{7}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step multiplication and simplification of an algebraic expression involving cube roots.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {\sqrt[3]{x} - 2} \right)\left( {\sqrt[3]{x} + 4} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
